camera_and_editing:
Shape match cuts (Bass grammar):
- the six title bars into six glasses;
- the wine ring into the coaster, and later into the blood pool;
- the card rectangle into the phone screen, the oven door and the back door;
- the line of light under the bathroom door into the line of gas haze;
- Angelo's failed spark into the first fire window;
- the title bars into the strobing fence slats at the end.

feasibility_and_risks:
THE BIG HEDGE. This direction can be built entirely in Canvas 2D, and silhouette is the strongest available answer to the brief's biggest risk, procedurally drawn faces. There are no faces to get wrong. Faces exist only as profiles and as Bass-style graphic eyes in at most 8 extreme close-ups. Silhouettes also merge where limbs overlap, so simple rigs read as seamless.

WHAT IS GENUINELY HARD:

1. Hands. The whole film is built on hands: crisps, dealing, the wrist grab, the hand circle, the shoulder squeeze, the clasp, the knife, the lighter, the final two hands. They need a hand-authored library of about 16 high-quality SVG silhouettes, drawn at 4x size:
- open, relaxed, card pinch, deal, point;
- wrist grip, left clasp, right clasp;
- covering the throat, limp palm-up, knife grip, lighter thumb;
- shoulder squeeze, slap-flat, tremble-on-shoulder;
- the Extra's right hand.
This is where most of the craft budget should go.

2. Walking and turning. Walks happen only in long shots and in depth, drawn on twos. Turns happen across cuts, as in Reiniger, and a figure is never rotated in 3D.

3. The hand-circle top-down: four linked arm chains plus an empty fifth corner. Build it as one bespoke, hand-tuned composition, not a general IK solve.

4. Fire and explosion. Particle soup would look cheap. Flat three-tone animated flame shapes, ember particles and the negative-inversion frames look designed rather than simulated.

5. The type engine is real software:
- per-glyph layout using measureText per character, with pair kerning;
- eight behaviour classes;
- set-plane transforms and depth sorting;
- knockout compositing.
Fonts must be force-loaded with the FontFace API before frame 0, or exports will silently fall back to other fonts.

6. Export performance. ctx.filter blur (halation, background defocus) is expensive at 1080p, so cache static blurred layers per shot. Rendering all 20,160 frames headless will likely take 0.1-0.3 s per frame, so 1-2 hours per full export. ctx.filter only works in Chromium and Firefox, so the scrub preview targets Chrome.

7. Readability. With no voice actors, the type is the performance for 14 minutes. Enforce the hold rule (max(0.8 s, 0.3 s + 55 ms per character)), allow at most three active lines, and test-read the assembled cut at full speed before polishing.

8. Telling speakers apart in group silhouettes. Four redundant cues handle it: typeface, colour, placement next to the speaker, and landing sound.

9. Warmth in Act 1. Silhouettes can feel cold. The first act's charm comes from comic timing on twos, Christof's marquee lines, Angelo's dealt words, Kay's lingering lip-mark letters, and the warmest palette in the film.

10. The Extra must stay ambiguous. The visibility ladder needs disciplined review: if any frame gives it an outline or an eye, the film breaks.

HOW IT LOOKS PREMIUM RATHER THAN CHEAP:
- strict compositional grids and symmetry, in the Bass tradition;
- posterised three-band light instead of soft gradients;
- cut-paper edge noise, frozen so edges are still until we choose to let them boil;
- rim-light crescents;
- halation on highlights, seeded grain, gate weave and a paper-fibre texture;
- motion on twos with real anticipation and overshoot;
- at most four colours per shot;
- sound doing at least half the work.

VIOLENCE stays implication: it is shown through shape (curls squared off against a wall, a ruler-straight line of light), shadow (the body projected on the ceiling) and colour (wine-dark pools). Each image is held under a second the first time it is seen. It hits hard because it is so clean, and it is never gratuitous.

characters.dialogue_treatment:
Bodoni Moda 700 Italic, pip-ink, printed on card-shaped slugs in card-stock with a 10% corner radius. A suit index sits top-left: spades for statements, hearts when he pleads. Each line is DEALT from his hand position: a 300 ms arc, a 540° spin decaying to a ±6° landing tilt, a 'thwip' and a snap. Multi-sentence lines fan out across several cards.
Fear progression:
- flights get shorter and landings sloppier (±14°), then the cards simply drop;
- after Deena dies they land FACE-DOWN (blue backs) and flip after 6 frames, a delay felt as shock;
- at 'Five. It's five.' a BLANK card lands beside his: the extra intruding into his voice.
From 'Scott. Please.' (the script's 'first time all night he hasn't sounded like himself') there are no cards at all: Bodoni Moda 400 upright, 44 px, card-stock white, centred under his hands, cut on and cut off with no animation. 'Sorry about your house.' holds like that for 2.4 s.

the_extra:
RULE ZERO: the extra is never drawn. No body, face, silhouette, shadow, reflection or outline in any shot. It exists only as a number and as traces, and the film's counting grammar is how we 'see' it.

1. THE TALLY. HEADCOUNT sits top-right in IBM Plex Mono 500, 34 px, two digits. It rolls like an odometer (220 ms, ease-out cubic) with a soft mechanical tick whenever it changes. It counts the persons alive at the address, and it is ALWAYS TRUE.
- It climbs 01 → 03 → 04 → 06 with the arrivals, then ticks to 07 on the second latch click, when no one has come in.
- It drops only for deaths, at the moment they happen and before anyone in the scene knows. Kay: three seconds after the bathroom door clicks. Christof: on the crash. Deena: on her last breath. Marina: one beat later, offscreen. Angelo: on the blast.
- It never drops for the extra, so it ends on 02, which is what it read in the very first frame of the cold open.
The audience learns the rule 'record = what we can see + 1' at the second click. Then the rule is turned on them: during 'six people apparently playing' the record reads 06 and AGREES with the picture, which means one of the six at the table isn't real. The picture lies; the record doesn't.

2. THE GAP. From the second click onward, every composition holds one seat-width of negative space at Scott's right (screen-left when he faces camera, with the 180° rule held all film). Everything belonging to the extra lands in it:
- the seventh pile;
- the sixth card;
- the fifth wineglass;
- the VOICE's words;
- the photographer's measured position;
- the grass where the final hand appears;
- Scott's out-of-frame right arm in the cold open.
A 4% tungsten-core warmth lives in the gap all film, so 'something warm' is felt before it is understood.

3. MISSING TIME. It only acts in frames we don't get: 1-3 frames of void #000000 (the only pure black in the film), and the time slug skips with a red 'Δ +00:02' flag for 18 frames. This covers the bottle arriving, the five fresh glasses, the phone being taken and returned, and Marina's disappearance.

4. HANDS ONLY, TWICE, AND ONLY IN LIGHT. Its hand appears exactly twice: the sixth hand coming down on the sixth card at the rim of the pendant cone, and the hand on the grass at the end. Both times it is Scott's own skin tone and perfectly clean, and the arm dissolves into darkness at the edge of the light. Light reaches its hands, never its body.

5. ITS VOICE. The six-face typographic composite, world-locked in the gap, drawn after post (no grain, no shake), cut hard on and hard off, tagged 'SPEAKER: UNATTRIBUTED'. Its sound is all six characters' voice-ghosts in unison: dry, mono, unfiltered, clearer than anyone in the room.

6. COUNTING AS VISUAL GRAMMAR. Everything countable is staged to be counted:
- six glasses numbered 1-6 in the opening;
- a tally-stroke title where a seventh tick is heard but never drawn;
- count-pans that tick on each stop and always over-run onto the gap;
- card piles with numbered evidence tents;
- Scott's caret blinks, which the edit counts;
- count ticks that each detune 7 cents flat, so counting itself sours across the film;
- yard fence slats grouped like tally marks;
- the five names on the call that won't keep their numbers;
- the call timer that closes the loop.
The payoff: 'Two.' is the only time all night a spoken count matches the record. The record has nothing left to count, and every overlay fades away.
